.TH ISOTONE-STATE 1 "2026-09-19" "Isotone 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
isotone-state \- read and write a sink's Isotone state
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B isotone-state
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B isotone-state
reads and writes the filters a sink is set to, either in the running daemon's
shared region or in the file it is saved to. It needs no PipeWire, so it works
with nothing running, which is the first thing to reach for when the EQ is not
being applied.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B show
Print the sink's current filters.
.TP
.B set
Write filters into the running daemon's region, which takes effect at once.
.TP
.B save
Write filters to the saved-state file, which the daemon reads when it next
starts on that sink.
.TP
.B capture
Read from the post-filter ring the app's spectrum drains, for checking that
audio is reaching the engine at all.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-sink " name"
The sink to act on, by
.IR node.name .
.TP
.BI \-\-band " freq,gain,q"
A peaking band. May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-dir " directory"
Where saved state lives, instead of the default.
.PP
Run
.B isotone-state
with no arguments for the full list.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR isotone (1),
.BR isotone-daemon (1)
